Phase 4.3 + 4.5 + 4.6 — keychain SecretBroker, headless CLI, quick-start docs
========================================================================
Phase context: Future-tasks Phase 4 items 3, 5 and 6 ("the desktop a
user can run"); item 4 (packaging) needs USER CREDENTIALS — see the
blocked-items note at the end.
Revisions: the Phase 4.3/4.5/4.6 commits on main (after d60fc1b)
Environment: macOS darwin 25.5.0 arm64, local dev profile, UTC

PHASE 4.3 — keychain SecretBroker (macOS Keychain / Windows Credential
Manager / Secret Service), env broker as fallback
---------------------------------------------------------------------
- dependency admission (docs/36): the `keyring` crate v3 (MIT/Apache-2.0,
  actively maintained), per-OS native features so no platform builds
  another's backend (apple-native / windows-native /
  sync-secret-service). Confined to modbit-providers::keychain.
- crates/providers/src/keychain.rs (new): KeychainSecretBroker —
  keychain-first lookups (service "modbit-core", account = the broker
  name the transport asks for, e.g. OPENAI_API_KEY) with the env broker
  as the documented FALLBACK (CI + headless boots keep working);
  store/delete/has for the settings path; a mutation lock; typed
  errors; values never logged.
- service namespace is env-configurable (MODBIT_KEYCHAIN_SERVICE):
  keychain ACLs are per-creating-binary, so tests/E2E use run-scoped
  services — a rebuilt binary touching an entry created by an older
  signature would otherwise block on a user prompt (observed and
  designed around).
- wiring: SchedulerConfig::from_env constructs the keychain-first
  broker for the whole daemon; the settings surface stores an api_key
  DIRECTLY to the keychain — it is never merged into the settings
  document, never written to the environment, and never appended to the
  event store; SettingsView gains has_api_key (the key itself never
  rides a message).
- desktop: the settings screen gains an API-key field (password input;
  "stored in the system keychain" indicator from has_api_key); the
  bridge schema guard bounds its length and routes it to the keychain.
- DEFECT FIXED (surfaced during Phase 4.2's E2E hardening): leaked core
  processes from earlier panicking test runs (Rust's Child does not
  kill on drop) were accumulating and, combined with stale keychain
  entries, produced 30s+ request timeouts; all E2E cores now use
  run-scoped keychain services and the stale keychain entry was
  deleted.
- PROOFS: keychain unit tests (round-trip incl. rotation, env fallback
  on miss, keychain-over-env preference) green on macOS with the REAL
  Keychain; settings_keychain_e2e through the REAL daemon with NO
  OPENAI_API_KEY in the environment: the run REACHES the model endpoint
  and completes ONLY because the keychain broker supplies the key — the
  fixture REJECTS any other credential; the durable event store
  contains ZERO occurrences of the key string.
- MUTATION CHECK: swapping the keychain broker for the env-only broker
  fails the E2E (the task cannot authenticate). Recorded; reverted.

PHASE 4.5 — headless CLI
---------------------------------------------------------------------
- crates/core-runtime/src/bin/modbit.rs (new bin `modbit`):
  `modbit run <repo> "<task>" [--json] [--branch <b>] [--db <p>]
  [--timeout <s>]` and `modbit settings [--provider/--model/
  --base-url/--max-turns/--execution-mode/--api-key] [--json]` — over
  the SAME SurfaceProtocol daemon the desktop uses (auto-spawns a core
  bound to the repo when MODBIT_DAEMON is not set; drain threads keep
  the core's pipes alive for the CLI's lifetime — closing them early
  breaks the core, observed and designed around).
- run: create → queue → start → poll the fleet to a terminal state →
  JSON report {task_id, state, repo, base_branch}; exit 0 on
  ReadyForReview, 2 on failed/waiting, 1 on transport/usage errors.
- settings: partial update through UpdateSettings (an --api-key rides
  the keychain path) and prints the effective values (key redacted;
  has_api_key instead).
- PROOF (live smoke, real repo + real daemon):
  modbit run /tmp/cli-demo "say hello" --json →
  {"base_branch":null,"repo":"/tmp/cli-demo",
   "state":"TASK_STATUS_WAITING","task_id":"01a08cc8-…"}
  TASK_STATUS_WAITING is the CORRECT terminal state here: the bare
  fixture repo has no provider/model configured, so the run parks in
  the provider-outage waiting state (docs/14) instead of pretending to
  finish — exactly the never-self-certify invariant (REQ-EV-0119
  family). A configured daemon reaches ReadyForReview (proven by
  settings_e2e's default-mode control which applies a real edit).

